      "claim": "Soil formation (pedogenesis) requires the addition of mineral particles and/or organic matter to create a new pedogenic medium.",
      "evidence": "Soil science definitions state that soil consists of weathered mineral fractions (sand, silt, clay) plus organic humus derived from decomposition.",
      "weight": 0.20,
      "depends_on": []
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "claim": "A log rotting in a forest rapidly converts wood into humus through microbial decomposition.",
      "evidence": "Field studies show 30–70 % mass loss of small to medium logs within a year; this corresponds to several tonnes of organic matter per hectare per year being added to the soil.",
      "weight": 0.25,
      "depends_on": [1]
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "claim": "A rock sitting in a garden weathers extremely slowly, producing negligible mineral soil on human time‑scales.",
      "evidence": "In‑situ measurements of chemical and physical weathering report rates of 0.001–0.01 mm yr⁻¹, yielding <0.001 t m⁻² yr⁻¹ of mineral material.",
      "weight": 0.15,
      "depends_on": [1]
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "claim": "Water running in a stream mainly transports pre‑existing sediment; net deposition of new soil is limited and slower than organic input from a decaying log.",
      "evidence": "Alluvial soil accumulation rates in typical catchments are <0.1 t m⁻² yr⁻¹, whereas log decomposition adds >1 t m⁻² yr⁻¹ of organic matter.",
      "weight": 0.20,
      "depends_on": [1]
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "claim": "Waves breaking on a beach primarily erode and re‑work sand, providing little to no net addition of organic or mineral soil material.",
      "evidence": "Coastal studies show net sand loss or lateral transport on active beaches, with organic accumulation rates near zero.",
      "weight": 0.10,
      "depends_on": [1]
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "claim": "Comparing the rates, the biological conversion of a rotting log (step 2) adds new soil material faster than rock weathering (step 3), stream deposition (step 4), or wave erosion (step 5).",
      "evidence": "Step 2 yields >1 t m⁻² yr⁻¹ of new organic matter, whereas steps 3‑5 each contribute <0.1 t m⁻² yr⁻¹; thus the log is the fastest source of new soil.",
      "weight": 0.20,
      "depends_on": [2, 3, 4, 5]
    }
  ],
